WEBVTT

00:00:00.286 --> 00:00:03.472
Mia, two containers of water
have the same temperature.

00:00:03.737 --> 00:00:05.949
One is small and one is enormous.

00:00:06.387 --> 00:00:09.168
Does that mean they contain the
same amount of internal energy?

00:00:09.917 --> 00:00:11.891
Forty degrees on both thermometers.

00:00:12.206 --> 00:00:13.549
Surely that means a draw.

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.099
The giant container looks
suspiciously confident, though.

00:00:17.938 --> 00:00:20.396
Temperature is not the
total energy in an object.

00:00:20.771 --> 00:00:24.553
For the same substance in the same
state, a larger amount at the same

00:00:24.553 --> 00:00:26.845
temperature has more internal energy.

00:00:27.465 --> 00:00:30.266
Temperature tells us how
hot or cold something is.

00:00:31.028 --> 00:00:35.231
So a thermometer cannot tell me the total
energy just by looking at the temperature?

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:36.747
Correct.

00:00:37.088 --> 00:00:41.855
We measure temperature in degrees Celsius
in this course and energy in joules.

00:00:42.507 --> 00:00:46.830
Internal energy is associated with
particles' motion and interactions.

00:00:47.234 --> 00:00:50.780
Heat means energy transferred
because of a temperature difference.

00:00:51.511 --> 00:00:56.003
Then saying an object contains heat
is everyday language rather than

00:00:56.004 --> 00:00:57.857
the careful scientific definition.

00:00:58.674 --> 00:00:59.453
Exactly.

00:00:59.742 --> 00:01:01.475
It has internal energy.

00:01:01.989 --> 00:01:06.840
If a warm mug transfers energy to a
cooler room, that transfer is heating.

00:01:07.282 --> 00:01:12.353
In an ordinary spontaneous exchange, the
net transfer is from hotter to colder.

00:01:13.109 --> 00:01:16.904
Cold does not sneak into the
mug wearing a tiny burglar mask?

00:01:17.627 --> 00:01:18.640
No cold burglar.

00:01:18.951 --> 00:01:20.536
Energy leaves the warmer mug.

00:01:21.065 --> 00:01:24.470
When temperatures become equal,
there is no net transfer due

00:01:24.475 --> 00:01:25.708
to a temperature difference.

00:01:26.002 --> 00:01:27.905
That is thermal equilibrium.

00:01:28.689 --> 00:01:32.604
But a fridge moves energy from its
cold inside to a warmer kitchen.

00:01:33.329 --> 00:01:34.137
Good challenge.

00:01:34.418 --> 00:01:37.607
It requires an energy input,
usually electrical work.

00:01:38.074 --> 00:01:41.506
Our hotter to colder rule describes
the spontaneous direction,

00:01:41.776 --> 00:01:43.426
not a ban on refrigerators

00:01:43.806 --> 00:01:47.768
Imagine particles in a solid
vibrating around fixed positions.

00:01:48.139 --> 00:01:50.475
In a liquid, they can
move past one another.

00:01:50.876 --> 00:01:53.462
In a gas, they move
freely between collisions.

00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:56.447
What usually changes when
their temperature rises?

00:01:57.267 --> 00:02:00.176
The particles get bigger and
start buying larger shoes?

00:02:00.945 --> 00:02:03.624
My particle-sized shoe
shop has gone bankrupt.

00:02:04.194 --> 00:02:06.557
Their motion generally
becomes more energetic.

00:02:06.903 --> 00:02:10.775
In a simple model, temperature is
related to average random particle

00:02:10.776 --> 00:02:13.089
motion, not the size of each particle.

00:02:13.818 --> 00:02:17.093
And more particles means more
total energy can be involved,

00:02:17.484 --> 00:02:19.255
even with the same average motion.

00:02:20.022 --> 00:02:20.600
Yes.

00:02:20.809 --> 00:02:23.560
Heating does not always
raise temperature either.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:27.900
During melting, energy can change
the arrangement and interactions

00:02:27.901 --> 00:02:31.472
of particles while the temperature
stays approximately constant.

00:02:32.224 --> 00:02:35.155
So a flat section on a heating
graph does not mean the heater

00:02:35.165 --> 00:02:36.745
has magically stopped working.

00:02:37.526 --> 00:02:38.085
Correct.

00:02:38.469 --> 00:02:40.519
Check whether a change
of state is occurring.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.218
Now predict what happens
when most materials are

00:02:43.219 --> 00:02:45.034
warmed and can expand freely.

00:02:45.831 --> 00:02:48.919
Their particles usually become
farther apart on average.

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:52.739
The volume increases, but
no new material appears.

00:02:53.509 --> 00:02:54.141
Excellent.

00:02:54.832 --> 00:02:57.152
Mass stays the same if
nothing enters or leaves.

00:02:57.556 --> 00:03:01.399
Density then decreases because the
same mass occupies a larger volume.

00:03:01.665 --> 00:03:02.699
There are exceptions.

00:03:02.982 --> 00:03:06.692
Water behaves unusually between
zero and four degrees Celsius.

00:03:07.502 --> 00:03:12.303
I should write usually, not announce that
every substance follows my rule forever

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:16.738
A metal surface and a wooden surface
have been in the same room for hours.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:18.870
The metal often feels colder.

00:03:19.389 --> 00:03:21.178
Does that prove its temperature is lower?

00:03:21.922 --> 00:03:23.342
My hand says yes.

00:03:23.746 --> 00:03:26.681
My exam survival instincts
say there is a trap.

00:03:27.483 --> 00:03:28.956
Your instincts win.

00:03:29.497 --> 00:03:32.408
If both are at the same temperature
below your skin temperature,

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.989
metal usually transfers energy
away from your hand faster.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.578
Feeling colder is not
the same as being colder.

00:03:40.391 --> 00:03:43.007
Because metal is a
better thermal conductor?

00:03:43.763 --> 00:03:44.350
Yes.

00:03:44.752 --> 00:03:47.600
Conduction transfers energy
through a material without the

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.147
material flowing as a whole.

00:03:49.573 --> 00:03:52.993
Interactions between neighboring
particles help transfer energy.

00:03:53.438 --> 00:03:57.336
In metals, mobile electrons also
carry energy through the structure.

00:03:58.151 --> 00:04:02.772
So writing metals conduct because they
are shiny could lose the explanation mark?

00:04:03.484 --> 00:04:04.048
It could.

00:04:04.448 --> 00:04:07.717
Imagine my dramatic teacher
tear landing beside that answer.

00:04:08.173 --> 00:04:11.748
Use mobile electrons and
energy transfer, not shininess.

00:04:12.275 --> 00:04:16.072
A saucepan can have a conducting metal
base and a less conducting handle.

00:04:16.851 --> 00:04:20.445
That lets energy reach the food while
slowing its transfer to my hand.

00:04:20.931 --> 00:04:23.085
But I still follow kitchen safety rules.

00:04:23.779 --> 00:04:24.564
Exactly.

00:04:24.875 --> 00:04:26.810
An insulator slows transfer.

00:04:27.054 --> 00:04:29.730
It does not guarantee an
object is safe to touch.

00:04:29.921 --> 00:04:34.257
Wood, plastics, and trapped air often
conduct poorly compared with metals.

00:04:35.015 --> 00:04:37.257
Why trapped air specifically?

00:04:37.634 --> 00:04:37.938
Why

00:04:37.938 --> 00:04:38.126
not

00:04:38.136 --> 00:04:38.672
a large

00:04:38.673 --> 00:04:39.073
space

00:04:39.078 --> 00:04:39.241
full

00:04:39.242 --> 00:04:39.361
of

00:04:39.362 --> 00:04:40.080
freely moving
